{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods","id":1560872,"title":"Do the Get Love, or Hate the Norse Gods?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-06-22T18:31:05-07:00","id":1560872,"title":"Do the Get Love, or Hate the Norse Gods?","content":"Because I'm getting some mixed messages here.<br /> <br /> On one hand, It's established that Odin and his pantheon are actually vampires, and have been feuding with the Get of Fenris for ages.<br /> <br /> But then on the other hand, a lot of the Get's spiritual gifts are named in direct honor of those very same vampires they've been trying to destroy.<br /> <br /> <b>Such as:</b><br /> <br /> Might of Thor<br /> <br /> Hand of Tyr<br /> <br /> Horde of Valhalla<br /> <br /> Loki's Touch<br /> <br /> Ymir's Sweat<br /> <br /> Strength of the Einherjar<br /> <br /> Heimdall's Blade<br /> <br /> Sigurd's Stride<br /> <br /> and Valkyria of Freya<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> That 'Strength of the Einherjar' is especially noteworthy, as the Einherjar are supposed to be Odin's hand picked warriors, that join him in the battle of Ragnarok.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29009-darkking","name":"DarkKing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29009_1.png","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-22T18:45:31-07:00","id":1560874,"title":"","content":"So the simplest explanation is that there are actual Norse gods, who spirits that mostly hang out in the Umbra, and that the Get of Fenris are generally antagonistic with. The important thing about the Get is that they respect their adversaries (as long as they're strong) so naming Gifts or powers after them is something they would do.<br /> <br /> Then there was also a vampire who impersonated Odin, claiming to actually be a god, and the Get hated that guy. To be fair though even among vampire Odin's childer, many believe that he was actually a priest of Odin rather Odin himself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":741,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","user":"AnubisXy","date":". 06-22-2026, 09:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-22T23:25:14-07:00","id":1560876,"title":"","content":"They do not like them at all, as of the Revised tribebook where Odin is filthy, accursed necromancer and the Aesir and Vanir are his toadies. It doesn't matter if they're the &quot;real&quot; ones or vampires or mages, they're the enemies of Fenris... and therefore worthy challenges to be taken down. If something is named after one, it's usually because they're styling on them... except for the Swords of Heimdall, who do it unironically and are therefore the shittest of shits.<br /> <br /> (Revised opted to not change overly much, and W20 didn't really have an original mission statement to change things unless they were getting updated for modernizing reasons, like Phone Travel turning into Signal Rider, since by 2012 landlines were on a definite downturn and everyone had a cell phone. If it had been allowed the freer hand of later X20 projects, more changes might have been possible.)<br /> <br /> ((Also, the Fianna stole Balor's Gaze from Balor, so the Get aren't the only ones on this grind.))","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-23T14:36:28-07:00","id":1560905,"title":"","content":"It makes more sense that the Fenrir DO NOT get along with the Aesir - hence the legend of Monstrous Fenrir being Obe of the Beasts of Apocalypse - though I suspect the relationship has steadily deteriorated since the Get stopped being born amongst those who worship the Norse pantheon (Resulting in fewer Homids trying to thread the needle between the gods of their forebears and the new, strange Spirit World in which they are immersed).<br /> <br /> The Lupus Fenrir, of course, have never felt obliged to give a fig about human pantheons.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-23T15:18:12-07:00","id":1560915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods#post1560872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because I'm getting some mixed messages here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely true. I blame it on the early writers not actually thinking things out in advance. Fenris is an enemy of the Norse gods, but the shorthand of the Get as Viking Werewolves creates a dichotomy.<br /> <br /> Another thing to consider is that the Get actually predate Norse mythology by thousands of years. The actual history of the Fenris totem has nothing to do with Norse mythology because Norse mythology was made up by Norse storytellers over the centuries (and some elements of myth predate even that because they come from the old proto-Indo-European religion) thousands of years after the Get formed as a tribe.<br /> <br /> The early editions imply the Celestines and Incarna are they only &quot;real&quot; &quot;gods&quot;. Pagan gods known from mythology are either euhemerized supernatural creatures (vampires, wraiths, mummies, historical members of the changing breeds, fae, fomori, etc.), or figments of mankind's imagination that lodged into the Astral Plane but who actually didn't do any of the things the pagan gods were supposed to do (though these beings may still &quot;remember&quot; themselves doing it, or ritualistically perform such tasks because that is their nature).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods#post1560872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On one hand, It's established that Odin and his pantheon are actually vampires, and have been feuding with the Get of Fenris for ages.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yep. In my chronicles I have most &quot;Norse gods&quot; associated with Odin be actual vampires. Odin himself (by whatever cognate name) is simply the ancient Gangrel the All-Father. Some of the other &quot;gods&quot; are either his vampire childer (like the Gangrel Valkyries) or other supernatural beings that allied with him. I once did a thread on this forum as using the All High as the basis for an antagonist faction in the game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DarkKing</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods#post1560872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But then on the other hand, a lot of the Get's spiritual gifts are named in direct honor of those very same vampires they've been trying to destroy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Most of those I think are simply badly named. I do hold that some &quot;Norse gods&quot; are better seen as jaggling avatars of the Incarna (Thor, for example, is simply Grandfather Thunder under a different name, or if you prefer a minor Incarna leader of the Sturm spirits that is part of Fenris's spirit brood), similar spirits, ancient fae, or some powerful mortal being once allied to the Get who is now dead, but still remembered.<br /> <br /> STs will need to invent their own &quot;true&quot; pseudo-Norse mythology for their games. I would have liked it if the tribebooks had done this themselves, but it's not too hard as long as you know the mythology and have some imagination. For example, in Norse mythology the children of Fenris (Skoll and Hati) chase and will eventually devour the sun and moon to destroy them and begin Ragnarok. In my chronicle, the Get of Fenris believe two powerful lesser Incarna of Fenris guard, and will later will merely &quot;swallow&quot;, Helios and Luna in order to <i>protect</i> them from being destroyed during the Apocalypse and regurgitate them later after the Garou have won.<br /> <br /> Whatever the &quot;real&quot; story you decide, it should be something that players can understand has been misinterpreted by the Norse.<br /> <br /> Whenever you want PCs or NPCs to encounter the Norse gods as we know them (maybe a player has a PC Mage or Sorcerer whose entire schtick is about worshipping the Norse gods), they run into the Astral Plane beings that merely think they're the real Norse gods because they formed out of human imagination and belief. That way you can eat your cake and have it to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-25T12:49:00-07:00","id":1560984,"title":"","content":"This is general touches on a tricky aspect of the Werewolf lore that I know I go back and forth on in my games/stories.<br /> <br /> So we know that the Garou and the other changing breeds are thousands of years older than most of the Iron Age human cultures they\u2019re associated with (Celtic for the Fianna, Norse for the Get, Slavic for the Lords/Fangs, etc.; Bronze Age for the Striders and Egypt), even without getting into the metaphysical complexity of whether or not their was a universal deluge/reality rupture circa 14,000 years ago.<br /> <br /> On the one hand, from a Watsonian perspective, this doesn\u2019t make a ton of sense. Classical Iron Age Norse culture, even if we are generous and expand it to include Proto Indo-European/PIE culture in general, should be a minority of the Get\u2019s millennia of history. We can grant that the homids who lived contemporaneous to it would have adopted certain concepts and aesthetics from their contact with humanity and this would then be preserved by ancestors and ancestor veneration, but even then that would make it only one of several homid cultures the Get would have ostensibly moved through over the ages, including everything from pre-PIE cultures to Bismarckian Prussia. This is without touching on how, based on what we know of the present day Garou traditionalists, contemporary human cultures are a source of scorn and Weaver/Wyrm anxiety (altho tbf we can also reasonably argue that perhaps this is a post-industrial phenomenon and that the old Germanic cultures would leave a longer imprint on the tribe\u2019s collective memory than Bismarck would). <br /> <br /> However from a Doyalist perspective, I also get that it\u2019s much harder to be explain the concept of a human-adjacent culture influenced by and influencing humanity for millennia that just-so-happened to geographically coincide with the old germanic cultures for several centuries than it is to say \u2018Viking Werewolves\u2019. Theory crafting about the particulars of the former culture is probably more conducive to theory crafting on forums or writing speculative fiction than sit-down-and-go roleplay.<br /> <br /> That said I do enjoy such theory crafting and speculative writing a good deal, so I have no issues engaging in that here (lol), but this is just my two cents on why the divide occurs in general.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11637-gryffon15","name":"Gryffon15","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11637_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"900"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-25T13:09:19-07:00","id":1560989,"title":"","content":"As mentioned, I still think it makes sense for Fenrir homids to be at least open-minded about the gods of their Human kin, but that the Get of Fenrir as a whole have historically tended to regard the likes of Odin with suspicion based on the established fact that Old Gallows would seem to be a powerful vampire perennially trying to chew them into gristle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-25T19:54:59-07:00","id":1561015,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Gryffon15</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods?p=1560984#post1560984\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is general touches on a tricky aspect of the Werewolf lore that I know I go back and forth on in my games/stories.<br /> <br /> So we know that the Garou and the other changing breeds are thousands of years older than most of the Iron Age human cultures they\u2019re associated with (Celtic for the Fianna, Norse for the Get, Slavic for the Lords/Fangs, etc.; Bronze Age for the Striders and Egypt), even without getting into the metaphysical complexity of whether or not their was a universal deluge/reality rupture circa 14,000 years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>(Checks Shattered Dreams)<br /> <br /> That answer, as far as W:tA seems to have been concerned, was &quot;no, there was not&quot;. The major event around that time was the rise of agriculture... and, hence, the Impergium.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the one hand, from a Watsonian perspective, this doesn\u2019t make a ton of sense. Classical Iron Age Norse culture, even if we are generous and expand it to include Proto Indo-European/PIE culture in general, should be a minority of the Get\u2019s millennia of history. We can grant that the homids who lived contemporaneous to it would have adopted certain concepts and aesthetics from their contact with humanity and this would then be preserved by ancestors and ancestor veneration, but even then that would make it only one of several homid cultures the Get would have ostensibly moved through over the ages, including everything from pre-PIE cultures to Bismarckian Prussia. This is without touching on how, based on what we know of the present day Garou traditionalists, contemporary human cultures are a source of scorn and Weaver/Wyrm anxiety (altho tbf we can also reasonably argue that perhaps this is a post-industrial phenomenon and that the old Germanic cultures would leave a longer imprint on the tribe\u2019s collective memory than Bismarck would).<br /> <br /> However from a Doyalist perspective, I also get that it\u2019s much harder to be explain the concept of a human-adjacent culture influenced by and influencing humanity for millennia that just-so-happened to geographically coincide with the old germanic cultures for several centuries than it is to say \u2018Viking Werewolves\u2019. Theory crafting about the particulars of the former culture is probably more conducive to theory crafting on forums or writing speculative fiction than sit-down-and-go roleplay.<br /> <br /> That said I do enjoy such theory crafting and speculative writing a good deal, so I have no issues engaging in that here (lol), but this is just my two cents on why the divide occurs in general. </div> </div> </div>Ancestors for the Get are probably something else. &quot;Those mewling mall-prowlers are at it again! Let us go teach them a lesson when the crowds have receded!&quot;<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-26T00:38:00-07:00","id":1561022,"title":"","content":"I think the age of the tribes is too frequently emphasized in the wrong way in those discussions.<br /> <br /> Cultures change. And they change fast. A few decades was enough for fads like white dress for weddings and dressing baby girls in pink feel like thousand years old traditions. This only get worse without regular written records.<br /> <br /> While the &quot;no-history&quot; stance from W5 is utterly ridiculous, the expectations of continuity from 14k years ago are greatly exaggerated.<br /> <br /> So, while their stories and stances may come from the dawn of agriculture, the actual details and motifs of the tribes are probably not only a blend of influences, but frequently way more recent than it seems. Gift and tribe names may be as recent as two centuries and no one would be able to tell from glyph records.<br /> <br /> Also, if the tribes are given source cultures, let's roll with it, this is how the game was built anyway. At some point, some tribes culturally concentrated around certain human cultures.<br /> <br /> This mustn't be a linear progression, history is messy and full of migrations. The Get may have culturally centered around the followers of Odin already in the Middle Ages. Indeed, as they use their modern names, they probably adopted those by the 12th century or later, not sooner.<br /> <br /> Let's also accept the cultural lenses the game gives us, just reframe historically. A tribe that looks too much like a given culture isn't proof that they were a part of that culture's inception, but they had cultural inflection points under the influence of said culture, and for now it stuck. Happens all the time.<br /> <br /> None of this changes the tribe's history, and its traditions, stories and relationship to the spirits being rooted in ancient tribal culture, but mean we shouldn't search for the modern aesthetics and names there. Or think this inflection point constrains the possible tribal past.<br /> <br /> For human kin following the Norse Gods: this is not how it works, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28339\" data-vbnamecard=\"28339\">Atticus Cleanclaw</a> . Garou culture <b><u>IS </u></b>kin culture. Most homids formally accepted within a tribe come from known Kin, and known Kin aren't regular members of local population, they're insular communities and families keeping their own traditions. Not immune to influence, clearly, but they are at the actual core of Garou culture.<br /> <br /> Otherwise there would be a problem: Garou would always be trying to indoctrinate almost exclusively teenagers with little reason to pay them any attention. This would be a radically different type of culture.<br /> <br /> But in the end, names are names. You can use the name of enemies for a lot of reasons. You can have contradicting relationships to the same spirit, idea or person. They may have adopted those Gift names in the last 300 years simply because they became easy shorthands.<br /> <br /> Venerating one interpretation of them through names at the same time that you hate another for religious and historical reasons isn't really a rare thing. It is, if you remember, exactly what a huge lot of Christian admirers of those myths have been doing for real in the last 800 years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-26T12:14:46-07:00","id":1561035,"title":"","content":"My mistake: I was thinking of human-born Fenrir and their relations in general and should have remembered that heart &amp; blood Kinfolk aren\u2019t really mainstream types.<br /> <br /> I admit to having only read a number of old core rule books, rather than being able to read every sourcebook.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-26T14:19:23-07:00","id":1561038,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods?p=1561022#post1561022\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Garou culture <b><u>IS </u></b>kin culture. Most homids formally accepted within a tribe come from known Kin, and known Kin aren't regular members of local population, they're insular communities and families keeping their own traditions. Not immune to influence, clearly, but they are at the actual core of Garou culture.</div> </div> </div> </div>My portrayal of kinfolk communities is usually similar to the communities in folk horror media. Those communities initially appear to outsiders like any other local community, although often with some quirky local customs. Underneath the facade however is the real culture. So Get of Fenris kinfolk appear like ordinary Norse pagans, Medieval Catholics, Early Modern Protestants, or heavily secularized moderns depending on the time period. However, an outsider who continued to spend time there would begin noticing subtle differences and would likely eventually figure out that the people have this completely different religion and values.<br /> <br /> It's why I tend to put my kinfolk communities as distinct separate entities that are capable of maintaining enough isolation to keep the Garou culture pure. Those tribes whose kinfolk population is more integrated into human cultures, like the Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers, have a much harder time doing so. A lot ends up getting lost. So a lot more of their tribes are a result of Lost Cubs and the like being found, and the knowing kinfolk being much smaller subsets of the larger kinfolk population which can get away with being subcultures or having family secrets and a culture of not revealing much to outsiders. I think that's a reason both of those tribes ended up getting involved in crime (organized crime families for the Glass Walkers, and lesser street gangs for the Bone Gnawers) as such groups already have a culture of silence when dealing with outsiders.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-27T11:25:02-07:00","id":1561058,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods?p=1561038#post1561038\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those tribes whose kinfolk population is more integrated into human cultures, like the Bone Gnawers and Glass Walkers, have a much harder time doing so. A lot ends up getting lost. </div> </div> </div> </div>I wouldn't be so fast to say that.<br /> <br /> Jews preserved their culture extraordinarily well everywhere they went. Not only in Europe, but last time I saw the Beta Israeli had been mostly proven right through genetics, they're an offshoot separated for at least a thousand years and probably more than two thousands, with no contact with others, yet are still recognizably Jews.<br /> <br /> All those communities had to adapt to life among outsiders and most in cities.<br /> <br /> To say the Glass Walkers and Bone Gnawers lost much compared to the others because city is to ignore the very culture being examined.<br /> <br /> Glass Walkers are cultural sponges on purpose. They never tried or pretended to stay free of human influence because this is the whole point of their culture. They're also the tribe with the most well recorded recent-ish history, and while the game doesn't focus on this, it isn't unlikely that they have records from as early as Rome using an actual written language.<br /> <br /> What was lost? To think the Glass Walkers don't know their past as well or better than the others just because they don't look the part is to ignore the very themes of the game, that include the error of the Elders in being excessively rigid (and as Anthropology was already well demonstrating at the time, probably equivocated) with their traditions.<br /> <br /> Blending is not losing. You can lose your past with your own changes over time, and you can preserve through intense exchanges.<br /> <br /> Bone Gnawers are constantly losing their culture because its core is survival, not preservation, and survival as a marginalized group isn't conducive of preservation anywhere. And they also blend, their culture is the culture of the marginalized, of the dredges.<br /> <br /> This is its own type of cultural environment that academia ignored for a long time, but that has been changing. The streets have their own communities and history and quasi-ethnicities that share and transmit and preserve.<br /> <br /> How much of old Glass Walker and Bone Gnawer culture was lost to time is far more complex to analyze than this, and the causes not at all straightforward. In the end, probably more than even they think, if our society is any indicator.<br /> <br /> Same inverted for rural Garou. They're not as static as they like to think, just less aware of the changes, as normal to such communities. They participate in the complex, multilayered process of cultural transformation and preservation.<br /> <br /> And curiously, the tribe most likely to have lost a helluva lot because they have less robust preservation systems and a problematic assumption of immunity, is the Red Talons.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-06-28T13:23:10-07:00","id":1561084,"title":"","content":"Something that can make for a rather substantial division between a tribe's culture and that of their kin is that the Garou actually engage in a rite of passage to be accepted by the tribe's patron and engage directly with an entire spirit brood, while kin are miles away from that, constantly. Much like cultural disconnects between military and civilian sides, that can produce a noticeable division. Especially if the ancestor-spirits make themselves known to the Garou far, far more often.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","user":"Saur Ops Specialist","date":". 06-29-2026, 04:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-03T03:43:52-07:00","id":1561228,"title":"","content":"True - I wonder how gnarly an emergency would have to be before a Tribal spirit sent warning to a Kinfolk and which Tribal totems would be open minded enough to do so?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-03T06:46:23-07:00","id":1561234,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1560872-do-the-get-love-or-hate-the-norse-gods?p=1561228#post1561228\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">True - I wonder how gnarly an emergency would have to be before a Tribal spirit sent warning to a Kinfolk and which Tribal totems would be open minded enough to do so?</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually this wouldn't be particularly strange, IF the most relevant individual to deal with it is a Kinfolk. This is just not a frequent occurrence.<br /> <br /> Garou culture is not Spirit culture. The totems have no notion of the Garou as more worthy of their attention or anything. They contact kinfolk and human shamans, appear in visions, overall they contact whomever they see fit, however they see fit.<br /> <br /> But Garou are part spirit, powerful and linked to them through ties that, while not totally unique, are thus made more powerful and more well maintained by the Garou themselves. Who also control more gateways to the Umbra, actively denying humans access to them.<br /> <br /> Totems certainly have a special love for their tribes, that's real. But the difference in attention overall comes far more from how much Garou have an easy time to contact them and vice versa than anything.<br /> <br /> And Kinfolk probably enjoy a similar advantage over most other humans, but still below what the Garou have, by far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-07-03T07:09:47-07:00","id":1561235,"title":"","content":"The Get do not like the idea of the Norse gods at all. They identify with the Fenris Wolf, an enemy of the gods, and at least one entity who claimed to be the Norse god Odin was actually a Gangrel Methuselah who Embraced a number of female childer in honor of the Valkyries.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg",